  "description": "An annotated English translation of Xu Xu's 风萧萧 (The Whistling Wind), the runaway bestseller of 1943. Narrated in the first person by a young Shanghai philosopher, the novel draws him into a web of wartime espionage among Allied, Chinese, and Japanese agents in the occupied city, and among three very different women: Bai Ping, a Paramount dancer secretly serving as a Chinese agent; Mei Yingzi, a dazzling Eurasian socialite in the Allied network; and Helen, a music-loving young American. First serialized in the literary supplement of the Sao dang bao in Chongqing from March 1943, it became the most popular book of the year, so much so that 1943 was dubbed 'the Year of Xu Xu.' This edition supplies footnotes, a glossary, and a translator's apparatus for the non-specialist reader.",
  "cover_image": "cover.jpg",
  "_source_note": "Source: a digital EPUB of 风萧萧 (simplified-character text; publisher metadata credits epub掌上书苑 / cnepub, dated 2020-09-17). No OCR. The novel proper is 58 unnumbered-but-numerically-headed chapters (一..五十八), first-person narration, ~236k source characters here. THREE source spine documents are intentionally NOT modelled as translated body chapters, and must not be treated as dropped: (1) coverpage.html — the cover image plus an uploader-supplied scholarly 简介/abstract (not by Xu Xu); the cover art is reused as the edition cover and the abstract informed the English catalogue description. (2) chapter61.html — a 45-char edition/imprint note (風蕭蕭; 徐訏; 出版社：成都东方书店; 出版年：1944年（民国三十一年）; note of the 上海夜窗书屋 1949 edition); this becomes the translated Colophon back-matter on the final batch. (3) chapter1.html — the source's own 目录/table of contents, superseded by the build's full hyperlinked TOC. All other spine documents are represented in 'structure'.",
  "source_epub": "source.epub",
  "_edition_note": "Original: 风萧萧, serialized in the 扫荡报 (Sao dang bao) literary supplement, Chongqing, from March 1943; first book editions 成都东方书店 1944 and 上海夜窗书屋 1949. Author 徐訏 (Xu Xu, 1908–1980), original name 徐传琮, of Cixi, Zhejiang; Peking University philosophy graduate; made his name with 鬼恋 (1937).",
  "_translator_note": "Rendered as the Translator's Note back-matter page. Numeric character references only.",
  "translator_note": [
    "This is an English translation of <i>风萧萧</i> (<i>The Whistling Wind</i>), the 1943 novel by Xu Xu (<span lang=\"zh-Hans\">徐訏</span>, 1908&#8211;1980), made from the digital source EPUB.",
    "Xu Xu&#8217;s title echoes the ancient parting song <i>风萧萧兮易水寒</i>&#8212;&#8216;the wind whistles, cold runs the Yi&#8217;&#8212;sung for the assassin Jing Ke as he set out on a mission from which he would not return; the register of doomed, high-minded sacrifice it carries governs the whole book, and the translation tries to keep that note audible.",
    "Names are given in pinyin except where a conventional English form is long established; each recurring name is fixed once in the glossary. The narrator&#8217;s philosophical asides, the espionage plot, and the wartime Shanghai setting are annotated for the non-specialist reader. Notes are the translator&#8217;s throughout unless marked as the source&#8217;s own."
  ],
